WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.614 --> 00:00:07.520
(electronic music)

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:12.960
- (Greg) Hey Wisecrack, Greg here!&nbsp;When making a short&nbsp;
list of “really funny things,” chronic despair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:17.840
and unrelenting anxiety probably don’t make&nbsp;
the cut. So why are so many comedians these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:22.400
days talking about their mental health? And more&nbsp;
importantly, why is it still so freaking funny?

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:24.240
- (Jake Wiesman) I knew that I&nbsp;
was gaining weight because my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:27.200
belt stopped fitting around my&nbsp;
neck the same that it used to.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:32.560
- (Greg) Indeed, over the last few years--and&nbsp;
especially since Robin Williams’ tragic death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:38.000
in 2014-we’ve seen more and more comics making&nbsp;
material out of their struggles with depression,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:42.160
anxiety, and other mental health&nbsp;
issues. Pete Davidson, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:46.080
has made a running gag of his mental&nbsp;
health issues on Saturday Night Live:&nbsp;

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:49.840
- (Davidson) Oh no no no. I was born&nbsp;
depressed, but it might make me feel better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:52.080
if I was on TV more!

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:56.328
- (Greg) And Aparna Nancherla has similarly used&nbsp;
her anxiety as fuel for some excellent jokes:

00:00:56.328 --> 00:00:59.680
- (Nancherla) And like if you don't have&nbsp;
Anxiety, the way I would describe it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:03.120
is like there's an edgy improv team in your brain.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:07.440
- (Greg) And John Mulaney has been cracking&nbsp;
jokes about his own mental health struggles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:10.240
and addiction issues since early on in his career:

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:12.000
- (Mulaney) I actually just quit drinking a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.320
month ago... (someone boos) oh I'm not&nbsp;
exactly slap happy about it myself.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:20.480
- (Greg) Meanwhile, comics like Bill&nbsp;
Hader and Bo Burnham have been open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:23.280
about their experiences with performance anxiety:&nbsp;

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:28.880
- (Burnham) To come to terms with your&nbsp;
anxiety, on the road... you know what I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:32.160
in front of thousands of people&nbsp;
a night, it's very very abstract.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:34.720
- (Greg) Now, stand up comedy&nbsp;
might not seem like the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:39.440
obvious medium for exploring mental health&nbsp;
struggles&nbsp; given that, for most of its history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:44.480
it’s peddled largely in quips, punchlines,&nbsp;
and gags purely for laughter’s sake.

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:47.280
- (Mitch Hedberg) I wish they made fajita&nbsp;
cologne, cuz that stuff smells good.

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:52.000
- (Greg) But this recent shift&nbsp;
raises some interesting questions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:55.600
For one thing, how did we get&nbsp;
from Bob-Hope-style one-liners:

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:57.680
- (Bob Hope) I went to the dentist&nbsp;
this morning and I only had a dollar.&nbsp;

00:01:57.680 --> 00:01:59.440
- (Man) What happened?
- (Bob Hope) I had to get buck teeth!

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:05.680
- (Greg) To comedians dissecting the most&nbsp;
intimate details of their mental health struggles?

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:11.040
- (Patton Oswalt) If I’d had a gun right here, I&nbsp;
would've just bought it up, one smooth movement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:16.898
Just going like, "Oh they have French&nbsp;
crust pizza" (singing, mimics gunshot)

00:02:16.898 --> 00:02:22.480
- (Greg) And is this change a good thing? Are&nbsp;
jokes about anxiety, depression, and other mental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:27.680
health issues, just a new way to get a quick laugh&nbsp;
out of a tough situation? Or is there  

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:33.040
much deeper going on? Let’s find out in this&nbsp;
Wisecrack Edition on Comedy and Mental Health.

00:03:33.267 --> 00:03:37.440
- (Greg) While it’s no longer particularly&nbsp;
shocking to hear a comedian joke about depression,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:42.720
it’s worth remembering that the recent focus on&nbsp;
mental health in stand-up is pretty unprecedented.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:48.160
For most of its history, stand-up comedy was&nbsp;
less about sharing funny confessional stories or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:53.520
observations on stage, and more about crafting&nbsp;
and delivering clever setups and punchlines.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:55.440
- (Steven Wright) My friend George is a radio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:58.560
announcer and when he walks under&nbsp;
a bridge you can't hear him talk.

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:04.880
- (Greg) In other words, comics in the early&nbsp;
to mid-20th century largely focused on laughter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:10.320
for its own sake, and didn’t necessarily ground&nbsp;
their material in their real-life experiences.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:14.960
Comics like Bob Hope--along with Jack Benny&nbsp;
and Milton Berle--built their sets around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:20.800
rapid-fire jokes.&nbsp;While their setups often made&nbsp;
reference to their personal lives, this was only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:26.480
incidental. The real goal wasn’t baring their soul&nbsp;
on stage, it was landing a surprising punchline:

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:28.320
- (Milton Berle) And when&nbsp;
I arrived at the airport,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:34.000
LBJ gave me a wonderful reception a 21 gun&nbsp;
salute. And I got the powder burns to prove it.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:39.120
- (Greg) The comedy of this era tended to be&nbsp;
escapist--more about taking a break from life’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:44.080
hardships than engaging them head-on.&nbsp;Key&nbsp;
to that comedic escape is something called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:49.360
incongruity. That is, deliberately creating a gap&nbsp;
between what the audience is expecting to hear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:54.320
and what actually comes out of the comedian’s&nbsp;
mouth. Philosophers as disparate as Kant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:59.520
Schopenhaur, and Kierkegaard have theorized&nbsp;
that incongruity is the main ingredient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:04.240
in generating laughter: We laugh when&nbsp;
something disrupts our usual mental patterns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:09.280
and subverts our expectations. The classic&nbsp;
example is the person who slips on a banana peel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:14.240
while walking. The expectation here is: This&nbsp;
person can walk. We are very used to seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:20.160
this. We are not used to seeing a person eat&nbsp;
shit instead. Thus, the incongruity. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:24.880
this bit has become so cliche that it’s&nbsp;
practically an expectation in its own right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:28.240
which opens up the floor for&nbsp;
new banana-peel-related gags:

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:36.000
In pursuit of incongruity, some comedians&nbsp;
got downright weird with their sets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:41.360
creating completely nonsensical material in an&nbsp;
attempt to buck the audience’s expectations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:46.240
Standup comedian Charlie Callas, for&nbsp;
instance, made a decades-long career out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:51.840
of interrupting his own stories with bizarre,&nbsp;
nonsensical noises and facial expressions:

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:56.502
- (Charlie Callas) Hello! Is this (honks)&nbsp;
Is this - (honks) Is this - (honks)

00:05:56.502 --> 00:05:57.040
- (Greg)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:03.120
Even more abstractly, 80’s prop comic Gallagher&nbsp;
built entire sets around smashing watermelons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:06.560
with a hammer and pulling large&nbsp;
things out of a giant couch?

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:13.815
- (Gallagher) Crap there's a&nbsp;
potato chip... A big old crayon!

00:06:13.815 --> 00:06:16.640
- (Greg)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:20.880
Hey, it was a different time! Audiences&nbsp;
attending these kinds of shows probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:26.320
weren’t expecting a lot in the way of substance.&nbsp;
But even before Gallagher’s watermelon smashing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:31.760
comedy was evolving to do more than just dish&nbsp;
out jokes. Beginning in the 1950’s and 60’s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:37.200
a so-called “New Wave” of stand-up comics like&nbsp;
Lenny Bruce and George Carlin began to push the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:42.000
boundaries of what could be said on-stage&nbsp;
by incorporating crude humor and social&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:47.280
satire into their sets. Carlin, for instance,&nbsp;
was famously arrested on obscenity charges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:52.240
following a televised routine about the&nbsp;
dirty words one wasn’t supposed to say on TV:

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:54.800
(George Carlin, censored swears)

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:59.920
- (Greg) In a similar vein, comics like Sam&nbsp;
Kinnison used their stage time to attack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:03.840
social norms and institutions&nbsp;
generally considered sacred:

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:05.440
- (Sam Kinnison) So, Michael,&nbsp;
if you ever think about getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:07.840
married, if you ever think you've meet&nbsp;
the right woman and you want to settle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:13.440
down, and change your life. Can you do me a&nbsp;
favor Mike? Remember this face&nbsp;- (screams)

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:19.920
- (Greg) More so than earlier comedians, these&nbsp;
comics acted as cultural critics, pointing out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:25.040
perceived hypocrisies. Throughout history, this&nbsp;
has been a major function of comedy and laughter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:30.720
As scholar Lawrence E. Mintz writes - the standup&nbsp;
comic acts as “our comic spokesperson, a mediator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:34.880
an ‘articulator’ of our culture, and&nbsp;
our contemporary anthropologist.”

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:40.000
- (Greg) More broadly, Mintz contends that, in&nbsp;
certain contexts, the standup comic is granted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:44.640
permission to express feelings that would be&nbsp;
difficult to share outside the comedy club.

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:49.120
- (Bill Hicks) You are Satan's spawn,&nbsp;
filling the world with violent garbage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:53.440
You are (censored) kill yourself, it's&nbsp;
the only way to save your f**** soul.

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:59.040
- (Greg) While performing, a skilled comic can&nbsp;
enact an audience’s frustrations with the very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:03.920
real difficulties of relationships,&nbsp;
politics, and so on. In this way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:08.320
they can provide insights into our shared&nbsp;
human shortcomings--insights that would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:13.200
be entirely unacceptable to share at the&nbsp;
company picnic. Still, for the most part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:17.840
New Wave comics tended to direct that&nbsp;
criticism outward, at the broader society,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:22.080
rather than attempting introspection. And&nbsp;
that’s what’s so interesting about the present&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:27.920
moment - which we will playfully dub the Nü New&nbsp;
Age of comedy. More so than ever before, comics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:32.720
are turning a Carlin-esque critical eye inward&nbsp;
to comment on themselves and their community.

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:36.240
- (Jordan Temple) Yeah I think it's hard&nbsp;
for black men especially to talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:40.160
depression because we have so many cool words&nbsp;
available to us to undermine how we really feel.

00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:44.480
- (Greg) As a result, they’re able to&nbsp;
broach hard truths that, for years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:47.760
have been off the table even&nbsp;
for the boldest of comics.

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:49.680
- (Daniel Fernandes) And trust me when I say this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:52.880
nothing is scarier than&nbsp;
having to fight your own mind.

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:56.320
- (Greg) But is this necessarily a&nbsp;
good thing? That brings us to one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.320 --> 00:09:01.760
the central conflicts in this Nu New Age:&nbsp;
Namely, is laughter A. the best medicine,

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:05.200
- (Patton Oswalt) Hey, uh where you know&nbsp;
everything you do is going to end in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:10.720
annihilation and entropy so what's the point? I'm&nbsp;
like, **** you're right, you win again depression.

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:14.400
- (Greg) or B. an unhealthy coping mechanism?&nbsp;

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:17.840
- (Chris Gethard) Depressed, f**** up,&nbsp;
looking for any excuse to not get help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:24.560
and the fact that some of my heroes were...&nbsp;
messed up was a justification for that.

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:29.680
- (Greg) That clip’s from Laughing Matters,&nbsp;
a recent documentary about comedy and mental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:34.080
health. In it, famous actors and&nbsp;
comics including Sarah Silverman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:39.520
Wayne Brady, and Rainn Wilson speak candidly&nbsp;
about the mental health issues they’ve dealt with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:42.800
and explore the role that comedy&nbsp;
has played in their struggles.&nbsp;The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:48.080
documentary explores the potential harm of using&nbsp;
comedy to escape one’s mental health problems.&nbsp;

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:52.080
- (Sara Benincasa) I think performing can be&nbsp;
a temporary high, but like all temporary highs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:55.200
it... doesn't last. And you can chase that high,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.200 --> 00:09:59.360
night after night after night... but eventually&nbsp;
there's gonna be a club that's not open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.360 --> 00:10:03.840
or you're gonna have a night off. And you're gonna&nbsp;
have to deal with what happens in the silence.

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:09.120
- (Greg) Like any other addiction, the high of a&nbsp;
great performance can temporarily mask the pain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:14.160
of depression, anxiety, and so on. But&nbsp;
it does so&nbsp; without actually helping to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:18.160
solve the underlying issues. And in&nbsp;
some cases, it can make them worse.

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:23.120
- (Chris Gethard) And if you are thinking of&nbsp;
doing comedy as a substitute for therapy...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:30.720
it doesn't work, I tried. I tried&nbsp;
for a long time. It doesn't work.

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:34.960
- (Greg) To put it bluntly, the documentary&nbsp;
suggests that comedy and laughter have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:40.000
unproductive and even destructive for many&nbsp;
comedians--a way to tune out from a life that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:45.520
falling apart. If that’s true, is it fixable? And&nbsp;
specifically, is there a more constructive way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:51.040
make people laugh, one that’s less about avoiding&nbsp;
painful truths, and more about facing them? To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:56.560
find out, we can turn to a guy who’s not exactly&nbsp;
known for his punchy one-liners - philosopher and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.560 --> 00:11:02.560
horse enthusiast Friedrich Nietzsche. In general,&nbsp;
Nietzsche had pretty serious beef with laughter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.560 --> 00:11:06.960
because he viewed it as a potential threat&nbsp;
to our quest for self actualization.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:11.280
As scholar Mark Weeks notes, Nietzche&nbsp;
thought the most important human pursuit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:16.800
was embracing our future, our desires, and&nbsp;
our power in the face of life’s absurdities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:22.400
For him, laughter undercuts this pursuit. Indeed,&nbsp;
according to Weeks’s interpretation of Nietzsche,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:26.960
laughter, with its ability to ground us in&nbsp;
the present moment by overwhelming us with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:32.240
giggle-fits and good feelings, can distract&nbsp;
from the all important philosophical quest:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:38.240
Constantly striving to improve ourselves in order&nbsp;
to build a better future. To put it another way:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:44.560
laughter has a tendency to lift us momentarily out&nbsp;
of time by cutting tension and poking holes in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.560 --> 00:11:50.480
stories we construct for ourselves. Laughter, in&nbsp;
other words, is a loss of the creative tension&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:54.800
that the Nietzschean hero needs to construct&nbsp;
a meaningful narrative of their life--a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:59.200
waste of a vital resource. While&nbsp;
Nietzsche is hardly an expert in lulz,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.200 --> 00:12:04.720
and stand-up comedy as we know it today didn’t&nbsp;
exist in 19th century Germany,&nbsp;he does have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:10.480
point worth considering.&nbsp;As we’ve seen, a lot of&nbsp;
successful comedy works by creating incongruity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:16.480
pointing out gaps between expectation and reality:&nbsp;
in other words, undercutting various narratives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.480 --> 00:12:22.080
we tell ourselves about the way things ought to&nbsp;
go. Charlie Callas does this by literally cutting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:27.600
himself off while he tells a brief story, and Sam&nbsp;
Kinnison does this by poking holes in the societal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.600 --> 00:12:33.520
narrative of happy, loving marriage. In various&nbsp;
ways, comedians have historically generated a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.520 --> 00:12:38.960
of laughter by disrupting the flow of personal&nbsp;
and societal narratives. Going by Nietzsche,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.960 --> 00:12:43.840
we might consider this a bad thing because,&nbsp;
while comedians are deconstructing old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.840 --> 00:12:49.040
and useless narratives, they’re not necessarily&nbsp;
offering new, more productive ones to take their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.040 --> 00:12:54.240
place.&nbsp;And to bring it back to mental health: in&nbsp;
Nietzschean terms, comedy has failed some funny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:58.960
people by offering them a way to temporarily&nbsp;
undercut the narrative of their own lives.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.960 --> 00:13:03.760
In the process, they can numb out and avoid&nbsp;
facing their own existential dread, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:08.560
mental health issues that might come with it. A&nbsp;
lot of existing stand-up comedy, in other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:14.320
confirms Nietzsche’s worst fears about laughter’s&nbsp;
potentially harmful effects on the human spirit.

00:13:14.320 --> 00:13:17.520
- (Steve Martin) Well excuse me!!!

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:22.800
- (Greg) We can’t help but wonder if Nietzsche&nbsp;
might have been more okay with laughter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.800 --> 00:13:27.280
if he spent his time joking with friends&nbsp;
instead of crying alone in the woods.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.280 --> 00:13:30.560
Which, hey, hey, hey, you do&nbsp;
you, Friedrich. Nevertheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.560 --> 00:13:35.520
his criticism raises a question: should comics&nbsp;
try to use laughter to confront their problems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:40.480
rather than to escape from them?&nbsp;And what might&nbsp;
such a new approach be? While we have no evidence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.480 --> 00:13:45.280
that any of the comedians interviewed in Laughing&nbsp;
Matters dig Neitzche, they do outline what a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.280 --> 00:13:50.160
more constructive approach to comedy might look&nbsp;
like - specifically an approach that transforms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.160 --> 00:13:54.880
their struggles into something of greater&nbsp;
value, rather than just fodder for laughter:

00:13:54.880 --> 00:13:58.560
- (Rainn Wilson) We all have these kind&nbsp;
of **** things that happen to us. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.560 --> 00:14:05.040
we can get through em, you know? We can get&nbsp;
through it, and transform it, and use it!

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:09.920
- (Greg) Trauma and tragedy, according to these&nbsp;
comics, are not merely things to laugh at,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.920 --> 00:14:14.480
even if it feels good.&nbsp; Rather, they are&nbsp;
the raw materials the comedian can use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.480 --> 00:14:19.200
to&nbsp; craft entirely new narratives, instead&nbsp;
of merely shredding apart outdated ones.

00:14:19.200 --> 00:14:23.920
- (Chris Gethard) You know, if you’re an artist,&nbsp;
who has a chance to say something that might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.920 --> 00:14:32.160
actually connect people org els people and you&nbsp;
don't take that chance... what good are you?

00:14:32.160 --> 00:14:36.880
- (Greg) This approach to comedy is distinctly&nbsp;
Nietzschean. Mark Weeks contends that, in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.880 --> 00:14:42.560
later work, the philosopher made a kind of uneasy&nbsp;
peace with laughter, by “challeng[ing] his readers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.560 --> 00:14:48.880
to will a new kind of laughter,” one which leaves&nbsp;
us both amused AND philosophically elevated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.880 --> 00:14:53.520
To put it another way, Nietzsche believed that&nbsp;
a constructive, self-affirming laughter was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.520 --> 00:14:58.640
possible, but had yet to be realized in his own&nbsp;
time. He challenged his readers to invent a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.640 --> 00:15:03.440
form of laughter that would support heroic&nbsp;
striving in the face of adversity, rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.440 --> 00:15:08.960
than denigrating such efforts as being pointless&nbsp;
and silly.&nbsp;And we’d argue that the standup comic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.960 --> 00:15:14.160
who uses their struggles with mental health as&nbsp;
material is helping to make that new laughter a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.160 --> 00:15:18.880
reality.&nbsp;When a comic crafts a good story about&nbsp;
their struggles, they aren’t JUST entertaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.880 --> 00:15:24.480
-- they’re also creating a “heroic narrative” that&nbsp;
shows them facing off against the real challenges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.480 --> 00:15:30.240
of being human. In the case of mental health, that&nbsp;
means the difficulty of being completely reliant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.240 --> 00:15:35.920
on a blob of electrified goo that frequently&nbsp;
malfunctions. In other words, they’re responding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.920 --> 00:15:41.120
to Nietzsche’s challenge by generating laughter&nbsp;
that brings audiences--and themselves--into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:46.480
direct contact with the past and the future,&nbsp;
rather than merely escaping into the hilarity of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.480 --> 00:15:50.880
the present.&nbsp;We can see this in the work of Sarah&nbsp;
Silverman. Like the other comedians interviewed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.880 --> 00:15:55.760
in Laughing Matters, she cites her difficult&nbsp;
childhood as a sort of origin story for her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.760 --> 00:16:00.720
comedy career. In this way,&nbsp; she takes a direct&nbsp;
look at her past, rather than running from it:

00:16:00.720 --> 00:16:05.200
- (Silverman) A hundred percent of&nbsp;
comedians become comedians because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.200 --> 00:16:09.680
somewhere in their childhood, they&nbsp;
needed to be funny in order to survive.

00:16:09.680 --> 00:16:14.720
- (Greg) Her refusal to use laughter as a&nbsp;
way of covering up past traumas pops up again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.720 --> 00:16:19.600
when Silverman describes what happened to the&nbsp;
doctor who prescribed her Xanax as a teenager:&nbsp;

00:16:19.600 --> 00:16:25.960
- (Silverman) The psychiatrist who originally&nbsp;
put me on it... hung himself. (laughs)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.760 --> 00:16:30.160
I mean I can't just skate by that, it's crazy.

00:16:30.160 --> 00:16:34.000
- (Greg) Notice how Silverman laughs&nbsp;
and smiles while telling the story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:39.760
but refuses to dismiss her psychiatrist’s suicide&nbsp;
as something inconsequential. She can acknowledge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.760 --> 00:16:44.480
the irony in the situation, and even chuckle&nbsp;
at it, without denying the traumatic effect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.480 --> 00:16:50.320
that these events had on her younger self. What’s&nbsp;
more, she explains precisely what that effect was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.320 --> 00:16:54.560
drawing a narrative arc from her past&nbsp;
through her present and into the future:

00:16:54.560 --> 00:16:58.640
- (Silverman) These years of torture&nbsp;
and shame kind of became my superpower.

00:16:58.640 --> 00:17:04.480
- (Greg) Silverman is literally framing her comedy&nbsp;
career in terms of a superhero’s origin story. Far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.480 --> 00:17:09.760
from avoiding her problems, Silverman embraces&nbsp;
them as a key part of her creative process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.760 --> 00:17:15.040
without romanticizing them as the sole source&nbsp;
of her funniness. Depression doesn’t make a joke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.040 --> 00:17:19.840
out of Silverman: Silverman makes jokes out of&nbsp;
depression. She demonstrates that comedians can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.840 --> 00:17:25.440
transform mental health struggles from a taboo&nbsp;
subject into something worth talking about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.440 --> 00:17:29.920
even celebrating, if only for the&nbsp;
duration of a set. Aparna Nancherla,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.920 --> 00:17:33.520
for her part, applies a similar&nbsp;
technique to the topic of anxiety:

00:17:33.520 --> 00:17:38.080
- (Nancherla) I feel like it’s weirder to not have&nbsp;
anxiety than to have it. Cus I feel like if you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.080 --> 00:17:42.800
not scared, you're not paying attention,&nbsp;
you know? Like I feel like if you open a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.800 --> 00:17:47.680
newspaper today and skim maybe three headlines&nbsp;
you're just like, seems cool! It's like what!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.680 --> 00:17:53.280
Everything's on fire! Even the newspaper's&nbsp;
on fire! What are you so chill about?

00:17:53.280 --> 00:17:59.280
- (Greg) Here, Nancherla frames anxiety not as&nbsp;
a failing, but as the only rational response to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:59.280 --> 00:18:05.200
a society that insists we post happy photos of&nbsp;
ourselves on Instagram while the world crumbles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.200 --> 00:18:09.520
around us. That’s not to say that everyone&nbsp;
needs to get stoked on their own depression&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.520 --> 00:18:14.800
or anxiety - but it’s one way to deal.&nbsp;Comics&nbsp;
can also transform trauma into connection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.800 --> 00:18:20.080
with other comics or audience members, or&nbsp;
insomniacs watching grainy videos on Youtube.&nbsp;

00:18:20.080 --> 00:18:24.000
- (Sara) It's one of the reasons&nbsp;
that I love being a comedian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:28.160
because if I can find comedy people&nbsp;
anywhere I go in the world, and I have,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.160 --> 00:18:32.640
I know I'm gonna hear good stories. And I'm&nbsp;
gonna be sitting around with people who are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.640 --> 00:18:39.840
also, if not ok with being **** up, at least&nbsp;
perhaps more honest than other folks might be.

00:18:39.840 --> 00:18:43.840
- (Greg) And what’s more Neitzchean&nbsp;
than transforming trauma into purpose?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.840 --> 00:18:47.600
By using their darkest moments to&nbsp;
generate laughter, these comics aren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:47.600 --> 00:18:52.320
just constructing their own heroic narratives:&nbsp;
they’re offering us the chance to create ours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.320 --> 00:18:57.040
too. In other words, if this guy can use&nbsp;
humor to overcome his mental health struggles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.040 --> 00:19:01.440
then maybe there’s hope for us and&nbsp;
our friends and our cranky coworkers.

00:19:01.440 --> 00:19:08.080
- (Chris Gethard) The real mission is if your kid&nbsp;
is sick, or your cousin is suffering, or the guy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.080 --> 00:19:12.400
on the other side of the cubicle wall shows up and&nbsp;
you never know which person you're going to get.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.680 --> 00:19:16.000
How can I make those people understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:16.880 --> 00:19:21.760
that that person's not being a dickhead that&nbsp;
day, and that person is not being melodramatic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:22.400 --> 00:19:26.000
and that person isn't going through a&nbsp;
phase, but it's because that person is sick.

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:30.160
- (Greg) By making comedy intended to keep&nbsp;
us alive and striving for something greater,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:30.160 --> 00:19:33.760
these comedians are generating a kind&nbsp;
of laughter which Nietzsche might be&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.760 --> 00:19:38.320
darn proud of--a laughter that serves&nbsp;
to construct, rather than to tear down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.320 --> 00:19:41.920
And that’s nothing to laugh at.&nbsp;
Just kidding! It’s pretty funny.

00:19:41.920 --> 00:19:45.760
- (Patton Oswalt) I don't want to be out in&nbsp;
the wasteland fat and depressed! Like that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:45.760 --> 00:19:48.160
there's not gonna be any&nbsp;
Prozac after the collapse.

00:19:48.160 --> 00:19:52.560
- (Greg) But what do you guys think? Can comedy&nbsp;
help us all become better people? Or should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.560 --> 00:19:58.800
comedians stick to smashing watermelons? Shout&nbsp;
out to Gallagher! Let us know in the comments!&nbsp;And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.800 --> 00:20:04.240
since we’re on the subject, if you or anyone you&nbsp;
know is going through a hard time, yo please reach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.240 --> 00:20:09.600
out and ask for help. Mostly because you deserve&nbsp;
it inherently, but also because y’all mean a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:09.600 --> 00:20:14.240
to us. And if you ever need it, you can find the&nbsp;
link and phone number for the National Suicide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:14.240 --> 00:20:19.520
Prevention Lifeline here and in the description&nbsp;
below. Big thanks to our patrons for all your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:19.520 --> 00:20:33.840
support. Be sure to subscribe and ring that bell.&nbsp;
And as always, thanks for watching, folks. Peace.

